# autofix-demo.otd — OTD7.1: located overlap errors + `otd --autofix`
#
# block_b is placed 5cm from block_a's center, but both are 8cm cubes —
# 3cm too close, so they interpenetrate by exactly 30mm along X. This is
# the easiest mistake to make in any scene assembled from separate parts:
# a copy-pasted `at (...)` that doesn't leave quite enough room.
#
# Run it as-is (strict mode is off below, so this is a warning, not a
# hard error) and the message now names a real 3D location, not just a
# distance:
#
#   otd --check examples/autofix-demo.otd
#     block_a and block_b interpenetrate by 30.00 mm — centered at
#     (25.0, 40.0, 0.0) mm, shallowest escape axis X — mass counts the
#     overlap twice until you fuse them with add (run `strict: overlap`
#     to make this a hard error, `otd --autofix` to fix it, or
#     `simulate: solidity` to audit)
#
# Then let the tool fix it instead of hand-editing coordinates: it moves
# both blocks apart along X, mass-weighted (the denser steel block moves
# less, the lighter aluminum block moves more), until they clear by a
# hair over 30mm combined, and reports exactly what changed:
#
#   otd --check examples/autofix-demo.otd --autofix
#     [fix] AUTOFIX: applied 2 fix(es)
#     [fix] moved block_a ... mm along X to clear block_b — now at (...) mm, was (...) mm
#     [fix] moved block_b ... mm along X to clear block_a — now at (...) mm, was (...) mm
#
# Uncomment the next line to make the overlap a hard compilation error
# instead of a warning (this is what an AI-driven pipeline should
# probably always turn on — a scene that's silently wrong is worse than
# one that fails loudly):
# strict: overlap

scene "Autofix demo — two blocks placed too close"

block_a = cube(w: 8cm, d: 8cm, h: 8cm) at (0, 0, 0) material: steel
block_b = cube(w: 8cm, d: 8cm, h: 8cm) at (5cm, 0, 0) material: aluminum

color block_a: steelblue
color block_b: crimson

simulate: solidity
